<h1 class="mb-3">Law Enforcement</h1>
<p class="lead">
  Malaysia has laws that protect victims of domestic violence and gender-based
  violence. These are the main ones.
</p>

<%# Domestic Violence Act 1994 %>
<div class="card mb-4 border-danger">
  <div class="card-header bg-danger text-white">
    <strong>Domestic Violence Act 1994 (Act 521)</strong>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p>
      This is the main law on domestic violence. It was amended in 2017 to give
      victims faster and wider protection. It offers three kinds of protection orders:
    </p>
    <div class="row g-3">
      <div class="col-12 col-md-4">
        <div class="p-3 border rounded h-100">
          <h5>Emergency Protection Order (EPO)</h5>
          <p class="mb-0">
            Issued by a social welfare officer, at any time including weekends,
            so a victim does not have to wait for the courts. It lasts 7 days.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-12 col-md-4">
        <div class="p-3 border rounded h-100">
          <h5>Interim Protection Order (IPO)</h5>
          <p class="mb-0">
            Issued by a magistrate. It protects the victim while the police
            investigate the case.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-12 col-md-4">
        <div class="p-3 border rounded h-100">
          <h5>Protection Order (PO)</h5>
          <p class="mb-0">
            Issued by the court. It protects the victim during the criminal
            court proceedings.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%# Anti-Sexual Harassment Act 2022 %>
<div class="card mb-4">
  <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">
    <strong>Anti-Sexual Harassment Act 2022</strong>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p>
      This law came into force on 28 March 2023. It covers sexual harassment in
      all areas of everyday life, not only at work.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-0">
      It created the <strong>Tribunal for Anti-Sexual Harassment</strong>, a faster
      and cheaper alternative to the civil courts. The Tribunal can order a public
      apology, compensation of up to RM250,000, or attendance at a programme.
      Making a complaint to the Tribunal does not stop you from also making a
      police report.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<%# Penal Code %>
<div class="card mb-4">
  <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">
    <strong>Penal Code</strong>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p class="mb-0">
      Violent acts such as causing hurt or sexual offences are also crimes under the
      Penal Code. A victim can make a police report and the police can investigate
      these acts as criminal offences.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<%# What a victim can do %>
<h2 class="h4 mt-4 mb-3">What a victim can do</h2>
<ol class="list-group list-group-numbered mb-4">
  <li class="list-group-item">Get to a safe place if you can, and call 999 if you are in danger.</li>
  <li class="list-group-item">Make a police report at the nearest police station.</li>
  <li class="list-group-item">Ask for a protection order (EPO, IPO or PO).</li>
  <li class="list-group-item">Contact a support organisation for advice and shelter.</li>
</ol>

